    <h1>2 Weeks in Thailand: The Perfect Itinerary</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">Bangkok, the North and the islands, day by day. The split I recommend most often, the transport between each stop, the budget to expect and the summer variation.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="container"><div class="article-cover img-reveal"><img fetchpriority="high" sizes="100vw" src="/images/IMG_6424.jpg" alt="Vue aérienne sur les îles de la mer d&#x27;Andaman"></div></div>
</header>

<div class="section-pad">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="prose">
<p>Two weeks in Thailand is the right length for a first trip. It is long enough to combine Bangkok, the North around Chiang Mai and a week on the islands, and short enough that every stop counts. The itinerary I recommend most often fits in one sentence. <strong>Three nights in Bangkok, four in the North, six on the islands, and a last night near the airport.</strong> The rest of this article walks through that plan day by day, with the transport between each stop, the budget to expect and the variation to choose if you are travelling in July or August. I have lived in Thailand for more than three years, and this is the itinerary I build most often for my travellers, adapting it to each of them.</p>

<h2>The golden rule before you start</h2>
<p>The biggest mistake I see is trying to do everything. Thailand is almost thirteen times the size of Switzerland, and every transfer costs you half a day. For two weeks, I advise <strong>three regions at most</strong> and three or four nights per stop. You actually enjoy the places, instead of living out of your suitcase. A six-stop itinerary looks richer on paper, but it is more tiring in real life and leaves you with fewer memories.</p>
<div class="callout"><p class="ct"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 00-4 10.5c.7.6 1 1 1 2h6c0-1 .3-1.4 1-2A6 6 0 0012 3z"/></svg>Getting the timing right</p><p>The best time to go is <strong>from November to March</strong>. For the islands, be careful: the Andaman coast (Krabi, Koh Lanta, Phuket) and the Gulf (Koh Samui, Koh Phangan, Koh Tao) do not share the same rainy season. That is what decides which variation you should choose, and I come back to it below. You will find the details in my <a href="/en/blog/best-time-to-visit-thailand/">month-by-month guide</a>.</p></div>

<h2>The itinerary at a glance</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Days</th><th>Stop</th><th>Nights</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1 to 3</td><td>Bangkok</td><td>3</td><td>A gentle arrival, temples, markets, street food</td></tr>
<tr><td>4 to 7</td><td>Chiang Mai and the North</td><td>4</td><td>Culture, mountains, elephants, cooking</td></tr>
<tr><td>8 to 13</td><td>Koh Lanta or Krabi</td><td>6</td><td>Beaches, snorkelling, nearby islands, relaxing</td></tr>
<tr><td>14</td><td>Bangkok or near the airport</td><td>1</td><td>A safety buffer before your flight home</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Fourteen nights, three domestic flights and no road journey longer than three hours. This is the Andaman version, ideal from November to April. In summer, swap days 8 to 13 for the islands of the Gulf, see below.</p>

{% endraw %}{% include "article-cta.njk" %}{% raw %}

<h2>Days 1 to 3, Bangkok</h2>
<h3>Day 1, arriving without rushing</h3>
<p>You will often land in the morning after a night on the plane. Take the first day slowly. A neighbourhood temple, a market, a good meal, and a massage at the end of the day to set your body clock. I book my travellers into a quiet area that is still well connected, never on Khao San Road.</p>
<h3>Day 2, the historic city</h3>
<p>The <strong>Grand Palace</strong> and <strong>Wat Pho</strong> as soon as they open, before the crowds and the heat. Then a boat across the river to Wat Arun, and an evening in Chinatown for street food, which is one of the best meals of the trip. Bring something that covers your shoulders and knees for the temples, I explain more in my <a href="/en/blog/thailand-temples-etiquette/">guide to local customs</a>.</p>
<h3>Day 3, a day away</h3>
<p>Two options. <strong>Ayutthaya</strong>, the former capital with its ruined temples, is an hour and a half away by train or minivan. Or an early-morning floating market, followed by a free afternoon in Bangkok. In the evening, a rooftop bar for the view, then a flight north the next morning.</p>

<figure class="article-fig"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/galerie/bangkok-rooftop.jpg" alt="Bangkok and the Chao Phraya river from a rooftop"></figure>
<h2>Days 4 to 7, Chiang Mai and the North</h2>
<p>The Bangkok to Chiang Mai flight takes a little over an hour and is often cheaper than people expect if you book at the right time. The night train is a lovely alternative if you have the time, but allow twelve to thirteen hours. Chiang Mai shows you the other face of Thailand. Mountains, crafts, more than a hundred temples in the old town and a wonderful food scene.</p>
<ul>
<li>Sunrise at the <strong>Doi Suthep</strong> temple, perched above the city</li>
<li>Half a day at an <strong>ethical elephant sanctuary</strong>, with no riding and no shows. I insist on the word ethical, and I give my vetted addresses to my travellers</li>
<li>A <strong>Thai cooking class</strong>, one of my travellers' favourite memories</li>
<li>The Sunday night market in the old town, and the lanes of the Nimman district for cafés</li>
<li>For the more adventurous, a night in <strong>Pai</strong> or a day trek through the mountain villages</li>
</ul>
<p>Four nights give you room for two big activities, a day in town and a free day. If you travel between February and April, be aware that agricultural burning affects the air quality in the North, with a peak in March. In that case, I shorten Chiang Mai and lengthen the islands.</p>

<h2>Days 8 to 13, the Andaman Sea islands</h2>
<p>The reward. After the culture, it is time for the sea. A direct flight links Chiang Mai to Krabi in about two hours, otherwise you connect through Bangkok. From Krabi airport, <strong>Koh Lanta</strong> is two to two and a half hours away by minivan, including a short ferry crossing. For a private transfer with no waiting around, my travellers use <a href="https://secrettravelandtour.com/taxi/">VIP Taxi Lanta</a>, which runs the route every day.</p>
<p>Why Koh Lanta rather than Phuket? For the calm, the long beaches facing the sunset, the village feel and how easy it is with children. And because it is the best base for boat trips.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Koh Rok and Koh Haa</strong> for snorkelling, the clearest waters in the region, the marine park being closed from mid-May to early October</li>
<li>The <strong>4 Islands</strong> tour, with Koh Mook and its Emerald Cave, Koh Kradan and Koh Ngai</li>
<li><strong>Koh Phi Phi</strong> as a day trip, 30 to 45 minutes by speedboat, to see the cliffs of Maya Bay without sleeping there</li>
<li>A kayak trip through the mangroves on the east coast, at Koh Talabeng</li>
<li>A tour of the island by songthaew, the old village of Lanta Old Town, the hot springs</li>
</ul>
<p>These outings are booked on the spot with a local agency, for example <a href="https://secrettravelandtour.com/">Secret Travel &amp; Tour</a> in Sala Dan. Alternate one excursion day with one beach day, that is the rhythm that works best.</p>
<div class="callout"><p class="ct"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 00-4 10.5c.7.6 1 1 1 2h6c0-1 .3-1.4 1-2A6 6 0 0012 3z"/></svg>Don't lock everything in</p><p>Book your island nights ahead, especially between December and February, but keep the excursions open. You choose according to the weather that day and how you feel. That is the luxury of a trip that is well planned but not set in stone.</p></div>
<p>If you prefer dramatic cliffs to a village atmosphere, replace Koh Lanta with <strong>Krabi</strong>, using Ao Nang as your base and reaching Railay by longtail boat. You can also combine the two, three nights each, but you lose half a day to the transfer.</p>

<figure class="article-fig"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/galerie/railay-eau-claire.jpg" alt="Clear water at the foot of an Andaman Sea cliff"></figure>
<h2>Day 14, heading home</h2>
<p>I never let a traveller leave the islands on the day of their international flight. A domestic flight from Krabi to Bangkok late in the day, a last night near the airport or in the centre, and you leave rested. If your flight home is late in the evening, you can spend the day in Bangkok without stress.</p>

<h2>The summer variation, July and August</h2>
<p>From July to October, the Andaman coast is in full monsoon. The beaches on Koh Lanta are still beautiful, but the sea is rough and boat trips run at a slower pace. At that time of year, I replace days 8 to 13 with the <strong>islands of the Gulf</strong>, which are drier in summer.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Days</th><th>Stop</th><th>Nights</th><th>Who it suits</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>8 to 10</td><td>Koh Samui</td><td>3</td><td>Families, comfort, easy beaches</td></tr>
<tr><td>11 to 13</td><td>Koh Tao or Koh Phangan</td><td>3</td><td>Snorkelling and diving, or a more laid-back feel</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Chiang Mai connects to Koh Samui by direct flight in under two hours. The ferries between Samui, Phangan and Tao take an hour and a half to two hours. The rest of the itinerary stays the same.</p>

<h2>Adapting the itinerary to you</h2>
<h3>As a couple</h3>
<p>The classic plan works perfectly. For a honeymoon, I keep six nights on a single island and choose one place with a view rather than two average ones.</p>
<h3>As a family with children</h3>
<p>I cut Bangkok to two nights, keep Chiang Mai for the elephants and the cooking class, and spend seven nights on Koh Lanta, where the beaches are shallow and the hotels are family-friendly. Fewer stops, more time in each. That is exactly what my <a href="/en/services/#formules">Family package</a> covers.</p>
<h3>Travelling solo</h3>
<p>Chiang Mai and Koh Tao are the two stops where it is easiest to meet other travellers. If you dream of going away without travelling alone, have a look at the <a href="/en/girls-trips/">Girls Trips</a> I organise in Koh Lanta and Krabi.</p>

<h2>How much does it cost?</h2>
<p>Excluding international flights, here are rough figures per person for two weeks in 2026, including domestic flights and activities. They vary a lot depending on the level of accommodation.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Travel style</th><th>Per person, excluding international flights</th><th>Typical accommodation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Budget</td><td>600 to 1,000 CHF</td><td>Guesthouses and simple bungalows</td></tr>
<tr><td>Comfort</td><td>1,500 to 2,200 CHF</td><td>Boutique hotels, a few resorts</td></tr>
<tr><td>High-end</td><td>3,000 CHF and up</td><td>Resorts and villas with a view</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For the detailed figures line by line, covering accommodation, transport, meals and activities, I send you to my <a href="/en/faq/">FAQ</a>. In the travel book, every stop is costed so you always know where you stand.</p>

<h2>The questions I get about this itinerary</h2>
<h3>Is two weeks enough?</h3>
<p>Yes, for three regions. No, for seeing everything. If you have three weeks, add Chiang Rai in the North or Khao Sok between Chiang Mai and the islands.</p>
<h3>Flight or night train between Bangkok and Chiang Mai?</h3>
<p>The flight saves you a day. The night train is an experience in itself, as long as you book an air-conditioned second-class berth several weeks ahead. Over two weeks, I recommend the flight.</p>
<h3>Do I need to book everything before I leave?</h3>
<p>Domestic flights and island nights in high season, yes. Excursions, restaurants and massages, no. Between December and February, the good hotels on Koh Lanta are full months in advance.</p>
<h3>Phuket or Koh Lanta?</h3>
<p>Phuket is bigger, livelier and more expensive, with a handy international airport. Koh Lanta is calmer and more authentic. For a first trip as a couple or a family, I pick Koh Lanta nine times out of ten.</p>

<h2>Shall we build it together?</h2>
<p>This itinerary is a starting point. Your trip will be unique. Depending on whether you travel solo, as a couple or as a family, we adjust the pace, the stops and the budget. I select the transport, the accommodation and the good addresses, you book yourself at the direct price with no commission, and you leave with a complete travel book. The <a href="/en/contact/">first conversation is free</a>.</p>
